"Basic usage - Composer (https://getcomposer.org/doc/01-basic-usage.md)\nciteturn0view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://getcomposer.org/doc/01-basic-usage.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 141\nL0: cite0†Home cite1†Getting Started cite2†Download cite3†Documentation cite4†Browse Packages†packagist.org L1: \nL2:   * cite5†Introduction L3:   * cite6†composer.json: Project setup L4:     * cite7†The require key L5:     * cite8†Package names L6:     * cite9†Package version constraints L7:   * cite10†Installing dependencies L8:     * cite11†Commit your composer.lock file to version control L9:     * cite12†Installing from composer.lock L10:   * cite13†Updating dependencies to their latest versions L11:   * cite14†Packagist L12:   * cite15†Platform packages L13:   * cite16†Autoloading L14: # Basic usagecite17†# L15: \nL16: ## Introductioncite5†# L17: \nL18: For our basic usage introduction, we will be installing `monolog/monolog`, a logging library. If you have not yet installed Composer, refer to the cite1†Intro chapter.\nL19: \nL20: > Note: for the sake of simplicity, this introduction will assume you have performed a cite18†local install of Composer.\nL21: ## `composer.json`: Project setupcite6†# L22: \nL23: To start using Composer in your project, all you need is a `composer.json` file. This file describes the dependencies of your project and may contain other metadata as well. It typically should go in the top-most directory of your project/VCS repository. You can technically run Composer anywhere but if you want to publish a package to Packagist.org, it will have to be able to find the file at the top of your VCS repository.\nL24: ### The `require` keycite7†# L25: \nL26: The first thing you specify in `composer.json` is the cite19†`require` key. You are telling Composer which packages your project depends on.\nL27: \nL28:     `{\nL29:         \"require\": {\nL30:             \"monolog/monolog\": \"2.0.*\"\nL31:         }\nL32:     }`\nL33: \nL34: As you can see, cite19†`require` takes an object that maps package names (e.g. `monolog/monolog`) to version constraints (e.g. `1.0.*`).\nL35: Composer uses this information to search for the right set of files in package \"repositories\" that you register using the cite20†`repositories` key, or in cite4†Packagist.org†packagist.org , the default package repository. In the above example, since no other repository has been registered in the `composer.json` file, it is assumed that the `monolog/monolog` package is registered on Packagist.org. (Read more cite14†about Packagist , and cite21†about repositories ).\nL36: ### Package namescite8†# L37: \nL38: The package name consists of a vendor name and the project's name. Often these will be identical - the vendor name only exists to prevent naming clashes. For example, it would allow two different people to create a library named `json`. One might be named `igorw/json` while the other might be `seldaek/json`.\nL39: Read more about cite22†publishing packages and package naming . (Note that you can also specify \"platform packages\" as dependencies, allowing you to require certain versions of server software. See cite15†platform packages below.)\nL40: ### Package version constraintscite9†# L41: \nL42: In our example, we are requesting the Monolog package with the version constraint cite23†`2.0.*`†semver.madewithlove.com . This means any version in the `2.0` development branch, or any version that is greater than or equal to 2.0 and less than 2.1 (`>=2.0 <2.1`).\nL43: \nL44: Please read cite24†versions for more in-depth information on versions, how versions relate to each other, and on version constraints.\nL45: > How does Composer download the right files? When you specify a dependency in `composer.json`, Composer first takes the name of the package that you have requested and searches for it in any repositories that you have registered using the cite20†`repositories` key. If you have not registered any extra repositories, or it does not find a package with that name in the repositories you have specified, it falls back to Packagist.org (more cite14†below ).\nL46: >\nL47: > When Composer finds the right package, either in Packagist.org or in a repo you have specified, it then uses the versioning features of the package's VCS (i.e., branches and tags) to attempt to find the best match for the version constraint you have specified. Be sure to read about versions and package resolution in the cite24†versions article .\nL48: > Note: If you are trying to require a package but Composer throws an error regarding package stability, the version you have specified may not meet your default minimum stability requirements. By default, only stable releases are taken into consideration when searching for valid package versions in your VCS.\nL49: >\nL50: > You might run into this if you are trying to require dev, alpha, beta, or RC versions of a package. Read more about stability flags and the `minimum-stability` key on the cite25†schema page .\nL51: ## Installing dependenciescite10†# L52: \nL53: To initially install the defined dependencies for your project, you should run the cite26†`update` command.\nL54: \nL55:     `php composer.phar update`\nL56: \nL57: This will make Composer do two things:\nL58:   * It resolves all dependencies listed in your `composer.json` file and writes all of the packages and their exact versions to the `composer.lock` file, locking the project to those specific versions. You should commit the `composer.lock` file to your project repo so that all people working on the project are locked to the same versions of dependencies (more below). This is the main role of the `update` command.\nL59:   * It then implicitly runs the cite27†`install` command. This will download the dependencies' files into the `vendor` directory in your project. (The `vendor` directory is the conventional location for all third-party code in a project). In our example from above, you would end up with the Monolog source files in `vendor/monolog/monolog/`. As Monolog has a dependency on `psr/log`, that package's files can also be found inside `vendor/`.\nL60: > Tip: If you are using git for your project, you probably want to add `vendor` in your `.gitignore`. You really don't want to add all of that third-party code to your versioned repository.\nL61: ### Commit your `composer.lock` file to version controlcite11†# L62: Committing this file to version control is important because it will cause anyone who sets up the project to use the exact same versions of the dependencies that you are using. Your CI server, production machines, other developers in your team, everything and everyone runs on the same dependencies, which mitigates the potential for bugs affecting only some parts of the deployments.\nL63: Even if you develop alone, in six months when reinstalling the project you can feel confident that the dependencies installed are still working, even if the dependencies have released many new versions since then. (See note below about using the `update` command.)\nL64: > Note: For libraries it is not necessary to commit the lock file, see also: cite28†Libraries - Lock file .\nL65: ### Installing from `composer.lock`cite12†# L66: \nL67: If there is already a `composer.lock` file in the project folder, it means either you ran the `update` command before, or someone else on the project ran the `update` command and committed the `composer.lock` file to the project (which is good).\nL68: Either way, running `install` when a `composer.lock` file is present resolves and installs all dependencies that you listed in `composer.json`, but Composer uses the exact versions listed in `composer.lock` to ensure that the package versions are consistent for everyone working on your project.\nL69: As a result you will have all dependencies requested by your `composer.json` file, but they may not all be at the very latest available versions (some of the dependencies listed in the `composer.lock` file may have released newer versions since the file was created). This is by design, ensuring that your project does not break because of unexpected changes in dependencies.\nL70: So after fetching new changes from your VCS repository it is recommended to run a Composer `install` to make sure the vendor directory is up in sync with your `composer.lock` file.\nL71: \nL72:     `php composer.phar install`\nL73: Composer enables reproducible builds by default. This means that running the same command multiple times will produce a `vendor/` directory containing files that are identical (except their timestamps), including the autoloader files. It is especially beneficial for environments that require strict verification processes, as well as for Linux distributions aiming to package PHP applications in a secure and predictable manner.\nL74: ## Updating dependencies to their latest versionscite13†# L75: \nL76: As mentioned above, the `composer.lock` file prevents you from automatically getting the latest versions of your dependencies. To update to the latest versions, use the cite26†`update` command. This will fetch the latest matching versions (according to your `composer.json` file) and update the lock file with the new versions.\nL77: \nL78:     `php composer.phar update`\nL79: > Note: Composer will display a Warning when executing an `install` command if the `composer.lock` has not been updated since changes were made to the `composer.json` that might affect dependency resolution.\nL80: \nL81: If you only want to install, upgrade or remove one dependency, you can explicitly list it as an argument:\nL82: \nL83:     `php composer.phar update monolog/monolog [...]`\nL84: ## Packagistcite14†# L85: \nL86: cite4†Packagist.org†packagist.org is the main Composer repository. A Composer repository is basically a package source: a place where you can get packages from. Packagist aims to be the central repository that everybody uses. This means that you can automatically `require` any package that is available there, without further specifying where Composer should look for the package.\nL87: \nL88: If you go to the cite4†Packagist.org website†packagist.org , you can browse and search for packages.\nL89: Any open source project using Composer is recommended to publish their packages on Packagist. A library does not need to be on Packagist to be used by Composer, but it enables discovery and adoption by other developers more quickly.\nL90: ## Platform packagescite15†# L91: \nL92: Composer has platform packages, which are virtual packages for things that are installed on the system but are not actually installable by Composer. This includes PHP itself, PHP extensions and some system libraries.\nL93: \nL94:   * `php` represents the PHP version of the user, allowing you to apply constraints, e.g. `^7.1`. To require a 64bit version of php, you can require the `php-64bit` package.\nL95:   * `hhvm` represents the version of the HHVM runtime and allows you to apply a constraint, e.g., `^2.3`.\nL96: \nL97:   * `ext-<name>` allows you to require PHP extensions (includes core extensions). Versioning can be quite inconsistent here, so it's often a good idea to set the constraint to `*`. An example of an extension package name is `ext-gd`.\nL98:   * `lib-<name>` allows constraints to be made on versions of libraries used by PHP. The following are available: `curl`, `iconv`, `icu`, `libxml`, `openssl`, `pcre`, `uuid`, `xsl`.\nL99: \nL100: You can use cite29†`show --platform` to get a list of your locally available platform packages.\nL101: ## Autoloadingcite16†# L102: \nL103: For libraries that specify autoload information, Composer generates a `vendor/autoload.php` file. You can include this file and start using the classes that those libraries provide without any extra work:\nL104: \nL105:     `require __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';\nL106: \nL107:     $log = new Monolog\\Logger('name');\nL108:     $log->pushHandler(new Monolog\\Handler\\StreamHandler('app.log', Monolog\\Logger::WARNING));\nL109:     $log->warning('Foo');`\nL110: You can even add your own code to the autoloader by adding an cite30†`autoload` field to `composer.json`.\nL111: \nL112:     `{\nL113:         \"autoload\": {\nL114:             \"psr-4\": {\"Acme\\\\\": \"src/\"}\nL115:         }\nL116:     }`\nL117: \nL118: Composer will register a cite31†PSR-4†www.php-fig.org autoloader for the `Acme` namespace.\nL119: \nL120: You define a mapping from namespaces to directories. The `src` directory would be in your project root, on the same level as the `vendor` directory. An example filename would be `src/Foo.php` containing an `Acme\\Foo` class.\nL121: After adding the cite30†`autoload` field, you have to re-run this command:\nL122: \nL123:     `php composer.phar dump-autoload`\nL124: \nL125: This command will re-generate the `vendor/autoload.php` file. See the cite32†`dump-autoload` section for more information.\nL126: Including that file will also return the autoloader instance, so you can store the return value of the include call in a variable and add more namespaces. This can be useful for autoloading classes in a test suite, for example.\nL127: \nL128:     `$loader = require __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';\nL129:     $loader->addPsr4('Acme\\\\Test\\\\', __DIR__);`\nL130: \nL131: In addition to PSR-4 autoloading, Composer also supports PSR-0, classmap and files autoloading. See the cite30†`autoload` reference for more information.\nL132: See also the docs on cite33†optimizing the autoloader .\nL133: \nL134: > Note: Composer provides its own autoloader. If you don't want to use that one, you can include `vendor/composer/autoload_*.php` files, which return associative arrays allowing you to configure your own autoloader.\nL135: \nL136: ← cite1†Intro | cite22†Libraries →\nL137: \nL138: Found a typo? Something is wrong in this documentation? cite34†Fork and edit†github.com it!\nL139: \nL140: Composer and all content on this site are released under the cite35†MIT license†github.com .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommand-line interface / Commands - Composer (https://getcomposer.org/doc/03-cli.md)\nciteturn0view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://getcomposer.org/doc/03-cli.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1015\nL0: cite0†Home cite1†Getting Started cite2†Download cite3†Documentation cite4†Browse Packages†packagist.org L1:   * cite5†Bash Completions L2:   * cite6†Global Options L3:   * cite7†Process Exit Codes L4:   * cite8†init L5:   * cite9†install / i L6:   * cite10†update / u / upgrade L7:   * cite11†require / r L8:   * cite12†remove / rm / uninstall L9:   * cite13†bump L10:   * cite14†reinstall L11:   * cite15†check-platform-reqs L12:   * cite16†global L13:   * cite17†search L14:   * cite18†show / info L15:   * cite19†outdated L16:   * cite20†browse / home L17:   * cite21†suggests L18:   * cite22†fund L19:   * cite23†depends / why L20:   * cite24†prohibits / why-not L21:   * cite25†validate L22:   * cite26†status L23:   * cite27†self-update / selfupdate L24:   * cite28†config L25:     * cite29†Usage L26:     * cite30†Modifying Repositories L27:     * cite31†Modifying Extra Values L28:   * cite32†repository / repo L29:     * cite33†Usage L30:     * cite34†Examples L31:   * cite35†policy L32:     * cite36†Usage L33:     * cite37†Examples L34:   * cite38†create-project L35:   * cite39†dump-autoload / dumpautoload L36:   * cite40†clear-cache / clearcache / cc L37:   * cite41†licenses L38:   * cite42†run-script / run L39:   * cite43†exec L40:   * cite44†diagnose L41:   * cite45†archive L42:   * cite46†audit L43:   * cite47†help L44:   * cite48†Command-line completion L45:   * cite49†Environment variables L46:     * cite50†COMPOSER L47:     * cite51†COMPOSER_ALLOW_SUPERUSER L48:     * cite52†COMPOSER_ALLOW_UNSAFE_PHAR_METADATA L49:     * cite53†COMPOSER_ALLOW_XDEBUG L50:     * cite54†COMPOSER_AUTH L51:     * cite55†COMPOSER_BIN_COMPAT L52:     * cite56†COMPOSER_BIN_DIR L53:     * cite57†COMPOSER_CACHE_DIR L54:     * cite58†COMPOSER_CAFILE L55:     * cite59†COMPOSER_DISABLE_XDEBUG_WARN L56:     * cite60†COMPOSER_DISCARD_CHANGES L57:     * cite61†COMPOSER_FUND L58:     * cite62†COMPOSER_HOME L59:       * cite63†COMPOSER_HOME/config.json L60:     * cite64†COMPOSER_HTACCESS_PROTECT L61:     * cite65†COMPOSER_MEMORY_LIMIT L62:     * cite66†COMPOSER_MIRROR_PATH_REPOS L63:     * cite67†COMPOSER_NO_INTERACTION L64:     * cite68†COMPOSER_PROCESS_TIMEOUT L65:     * cite69†COMPOSER_ROOT_VERSION L66:     * cite70†COMPOSER_VENDOR_DIR L67:     * cite71†COMPOSER_RUNTIME_ENV L68:     * cite72†http_proxy or HTTP_PROXY L69:     * cite73†HTTP_PROXY_REQUEST_FULLURI L70:     * cite74†HTTPS_PROXY_REQUEST_FULLURI L71:     * cite75†no_proxy or NO_PROXY L72:     * cite76†COMPOSER_MAX_PARALLEL_HTTP L73:     * cite77†COMPOSER_MAX_PARALLEL_PROCESSES L74:     * cite78†COMPOSER_IPRESOLVE L75:     * cite79†COMPOSER_SELF_UPDATE_TARGET L76:     * cite80†COMPOSER_DISABLE_NETWORK L77:     * cite81†COMPOSER_DEBUG_EVENTS L78:     * cite82†COMPOSER_SKIP_SCRIPTS L79:     * cite83†COMPOSER_NO_AUDIT L80:     * cite84†COMPOSER_AUDIT_ABANDONED L81:     * cite85†COMPOSER_POLICY L82:     * cite86†COMPOSER_NO_BLOCKING L83:     * cite87†COMPOSER_NO_SECURITY_BLOCKING L84:     * cite88†COMPOSER_POLICY_ADVISORIES_BLOCK L85:     * cite89†COMPOSER_POLICY_MALWARE_BLOCK L86:     * cite90†COMPOSER_POLICY_ABANDONED_BLOCK L87:     * cite91†COMPOSER_SECURITY_BLOCKING_ABANDONED L88:     * cite92†COMPOSER_NO_DEV L89:     * cite93†COMPOSER_PREFER_STABLE L90:     * cite94†COMPOSER_PREFER_LOWEST L91:     * cite95†COMPOSER_PREFER_DEV_OVER_PRERELEASE L92:     * cite96†COMPOSER_MINIMAL_CHANGES L93:     * cite97†COMPOSER_IGNORE_PLATFORM_REQ or COMPOSER_IGNORE_PLATFORM_REQS L94:     * cite98†COMPOSER_WITH_DEPENDENCIES L95:     * cite99†COMPOSER_WITH_ALL_DEPENDENCIES L96:     * cite100†SHELL_VERBOSITY L97: # Command-line interface / Commandscite101†# L98: \nL99: You've already learned how to use the command-line interface to do some things. This chapter documents all the available commands.\nL100: \nL101: To get help from the command-line, call `composer` or `composer list` to see the complete list of commands, then `--help` combined with any of those can give you more information.\nL102: \nL103: As Composer uses cite102†symfony/console†github.com you can call commands by short name if it's not ambiguous.\nL104: \nL105:     `php composer.phar dump`\nL106: calls `composer dump-autoload`.\nL107: ## Bash Completionscite5†# L108: \nL109: To install bash completions you can run `composer completion bash > completion.bash`. This will create a `completion.bash` file in the current directory.\nL110: \nL111: Then execute `source completion.bash` to enable it in the current terminal session.\nL112: \nL113: Move and rename the `completion.bash` file to `/etc/bash_completion.d/composer` to make it load automatically in new terminals.\nL114: ## Global Optionscite6†# L115: \nL116: The following options are available with every command:\nL117:   * --verbose (-v): Increase verbosity of messages.\nL118:   * --help (-h): Display help information.\nL119:   * --quiet (-q): Do not output any message.\nL120:   * --no-interaction (-n): Do not ask any interactive question.\nL121:   * --no-plugins: Disables plugins.\nL122:   * --no-scripts: Skips execution of scripts defined in `composer.json`.\nL123:   * --no-cache: Disables the use of the cache directory. Same as setting the COMPOSER_CACHE_DIR env var to /dev/null (or NUL on Windows).\nL124:   * --working-dir (-d): If specified, use the given directory as working directory.\nL125:   * --profile: Display timing and memory usage information\nL126:   * --ansi: Force ANSI output.\nL127:   * --no-ansi: Disable ANSI output.\nL128:   * --version (-V): Display this application version.\nL129: ## Process Exit Codescite7†# L130: \nL131:   * 0: OK\nL132:   * 1: Generic/unknown error code\nL133:   * 2: Dependency solving error code\nL134: \nL135: ## initcite8†# L136: \nL137: In the cite103†Libraries chapter we looked at how to create a `composer.json` by hand. There is also an `init` command available to do this.\nL138: \nL139: When you run the command it will interactively ask you to fill in the fields, while using some smart defaults.\nL140: \nL141:     `php composer.phar init`\nL142: ### Options\nL143:   * --name: Name of the package.\nL144:   * --description: Description of the package.\nL145:   * --author: Author name of the package.\nL146:   * --type: Type of package.\nL147:   * --homepage: Homepage of the package.\nL148:   * --require: Package to require with a version constraint. Should be in format `foo/bar:1.0.0`.\nL149:   * --require-dev: Development requirements, see --require.\nL150:   * --stability (-s): Value for the `minimum-stability` field.\nL151:   * --license (-l): License of package.\nL152:   * --repository: Provide one (or more) custom repositories. They will be stored in the generated composer.json, and used for auto-completion when prompting for the list of requires. Every repository can be either an HTTP URL pointing to a `composer` repository or a JSON string which is similar to what the cite104†repositories key accepts.\nL153:   * --autoload (-a): Add a PSR-4 autoload mapping to the composer.json. Automatically maps your package's namespace to the provided directory. (Expects a relative path, e.g. src/) See also cite105†PSR-4 autoload .\nL154: ## install / icite9†# L155: \nL156: The `install` command reads the `composer.json` file from the current directory, resolves the dependencies, and installs them into `vendor`.\nL157: \nL158:     `php composer.phar install`\nL159: \nL160: If there is a `composer.lock` file in the current directory, it will use the exact versions from there instead of resolving them. This ensures that everyone using the library will get the same versions of the dependencies.\nL161: If there is no `composer.lock` file, Composer will create one after dependency resolution.\nL162: ### Options\nL163:   * --prefer-install: There are two ways of downloading a package: `source` and `dist`. Composer uses `dist` by default. If you pass `--prefer-install=source` (or `--prefer-source`) Composer will install from `source` if there is one. This is useful if you want to make a bugfix to a project and get a local git clone of the dependency directly. To get the legacy behavior where Composer use `source` automatically for dev versions of packages, use `--prefer-install=auto`.\nL164: See also cite106†config.preferred-install . Passing this flag will override the config value.\nL165:   * --dry-run: If you want to run through an installation without actually installing a package, you can use `--dry-run`. This will simulate the installation and show you what would happen.\nL166:   * --download-only: Download only, do not install packages.\nL167:   * --dev: Install packages listed in `require-dev` (this is the default behavior).\nL168:   * --no-dev: Skip installing packages listed in `require-dev`. The autoloader generation skips the `autoload-dev` rules. Also see cite92†COMPOSER_NO_DEV .\nL169:   * --no-autoloader: Skips autoloader generation.\nL170:   * --no-progress: Removes the progress display that can mess with some terminals or scripts which don't handle backspace characters.\nL171:   * --audit: Run an audit after installation is complete.\nL172:   * --audit-format: Audit output format. Must be \"table\", \"plain\", \"json\", or \"summary\" (default).\nL173:   * --no-security-blocking: DEPRECATED, use `--no-blocking` instead. Allows installing packages with security advisories or that are abandoned. Also see cite87†COMPOSER_NO_SECURITY_BLOCKING .\nL174:   * --no-blocking: Disables all policy based dependency blocking during this command. Also see cite86†COMPOSER_NO_BLOCKING .\nL175:   * --optimize-autoloader (-o): Convert PSR-0/4 autoloading to classmap to get a faster autoloader. This is recommended especially for production, but can take a bit of time to run so it is currently not done by default.\nL176:   * --classmap-authoritative (-a): Autoload classes from the classmap only. Implicitly enables `--optimize-autoloader`.\nL177:   * --strict-psr-autoloader: Return a failed exit code (6) if PSR-4 or PSR-0 mapping errors are present in the current project (dependencies excluded). Requires `--optimize-autoloader` to work.\nL178:   * --apcu-autoloader: Use APCu to cache found/not-found classes.\nL179:   * --apcu-autoloader-prefix: Use a custom prefix for the APCu autoloader cache. Implicitly enables `--apcu-autoloader`.\nL180:   * --ignore-platform-reqs: ignore all platform requirements (`php`, `hhvm`, `lib-*` and `ext-*`) and force the installation even if the local machine does not fulfill these. See also the cite107†`platform` config option.\nL181:   * --ignore-platform-req: ignore a specific platform requirement(`php`, `hhvm`, `lib-*` and `ext-*`) and force the installation even if the local machine does not fulfill it. Multiple requirements can be ignored via wildcard. Appending a `+` makes it only ignore the upper-bound of the requirements. For example, if a package requires `php: ^7`, then the option `--ignore-platform-req=php+` would allow installing on PHP 8, but installation on PHP 5.6 would still fail.\nL182: ## update / u / upgradecite10†# L183: \nL184: In order to get the latest versions of the dependencies and to update the `composer.lock` file, you should use the `update` command. This command is also aliased as `upgrade` as it does the same as `upgrade` does if you are thinking of `apt-get` or similar package managers.\nL185: \nL186:     `php composer.phar update`\nL187: \nL188: This will resolve all dependencies of the project and write the exact versions into `composer.lock`.\nL189: If you only want to update a few packages and not all, you can list them as such:\nL190: \nL191:     `php composer.phar update vendor/package vendor/package2`\nL192: \nL193: You can also use wildcards to update a bunch of packages at once:\nL194: \nL195:     `php composer.phar update \"vendor/*\"`\nL196: \nL197: If you want to downgrade a package to a specific version without changing your composer.json you can use `--with` and provide a custom version constraint:\nL198: \nL199:     `php composer.phar update --with vendor/package:2.0.1`\nL200: Note that with the above all packages will be updated. If you only want to update the package(s) for which you provide custom constraints using `--with`, you can skip `--with` and instead use constraints with the partial update syntax:\nL201: \nL202:     `php composer.phar update vendor/package:2.0.1 vendor/package2:3.0.*`\nL203: > Note: For packages also required in your composer.json the custom constraint must be a subset of the existing constraint. The composer.json constraints still apply and the composer.json is not modified by these temporary update constraints.\nL204: ### Options\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRepositories - Composer (https://getcomposer.org/doc/05-repositories.md)\nciteturn0view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://getcomposer.org/doc/05-repositories.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 630\n---------------------------------